	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Remote Link uses your local network to find and control your computer.</string>
	<!-- The camera is used by one screen, for one thing: reading the pairing
	     code your computer shows. Nothing is recorded or sent anywhere. -->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Remote Link uses the camera to scan the pairing code shown on your computer.</string>
	<!-- The photo picker runs out of process and needs no permission on iOS 14
	     and later, which is why it was chosen. This string is here for the
	     older path image_picker still falls back to, where the prompt appears
	     and an app without a usage description is terminated rather than
	     denied. -->
	<!-- Add-only access, which is the narrower of the two photo permissions and
	     the only one this app needs: a file your computer sends is written to
	     the camera roll, and nothing here ever reads an existing photo. iOS
	     terminates an app that writes to the library without this string rather
	     than denying the write, so its absence is a crash, not a refusal. -->
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Remote Link saves photos and videos your computer sends into your photo library.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Remote Link needs access to your photos so you can send them to your computer.</string>
